Consider the following three ANSI-C programs:

**P1**
```c
#include <stdio.h>
int a = 5;
int main() {
  int a = 7;
  return 0;
}
```

**P2**
```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
  int a = 5;
  int a = 7;
  return 0;
}
```

**P3**
```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
  int a = 5;
  float a = 7;
  return 0;
}
```

Which one of the following statements is true?

Consider three processes $P_1$, $P_2$, and $P_3$ running identical code. $A$ and $B$ are binary semaphores initialized to 1 and 0, respectively. $X$ is a shared variable initialized to 0. Each line is executed atomically.

```text
Wait(A);
Print(*);
X = X + 1;
If (X == 2) {
    Print($);
    Signal(B);
}
Signal(A);
Wait(B);
Print(#);
Signal(B);
```

Any of the three processes may execute first, and context switches can happen at any time and in any order. Which of the following output patterns are possible?

Consider the following ANSI-C program:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
  int *ptr, a, b, c;
  a = 5; b = 11; c = 20;
  ptr = &a; *ptr = c; ptr = &c;
  a = *(&b); c = *ptr - a;
  printf("%d", c);
  return 0;
}
```

What is the output of the program? (Answer in integer.)

**Note:** Assume that the program compiles and runs successfully.

Consider the following ANSI-C function:

```c
int func(int start, int end) {
  int length = end + 1 - start;
  if ((length < 1) || (start < 0) || (end < 0)) {
    return 0;
  }
  if (length % 3 == 0) {
    return func(start + 1, end);
  } else if (length % 3 == 1) {
    return 1 + func(start, end - 1);
  } else {
    return func(start + 2, end);
  }
}
```

What is the maximum value that can be returned from this function? (Answer in integer.)

**Note:** Ignore syntax errors (if any) in the function.
